/**

@page tutorials_exahype2_dsl_implementing_kernels Implementing New Kernels

See page \ref documentation_exahype2_dsl for details on writing the timestep function for a new
kernel. This page details how to use this function in a solver. The implementation of the Rusanov
kernel is used as an example throughout.

# Generating C++ Functions

Each category of solver uses a python script to generate any required code, usually a file called
kernels.py. For the Rusanov kernel this file is python/exahype2/solvers/fv/rusanov/kernels.py. Create
a function in this file which is used to generate the C++ files from the DSL. Since the timestep
function must be specified for each dimension, generating a base header file which checks the dimension
is useful.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
def create_rusanov_kernel_definitions():
  if not os.path.exists("kernels"):
      os.makedirs("kernels")
  
  file = open("kernels/rusanov.h", "w")
  file.write("""#if Dimensions == 2
#include "rusanov2d.h"
#elif Dimensions == 3
#include "rusanov3d.h"
#endif
""")
  
  create_rusanov_kernel_definitions_2d()
  create_rusanov_kernel_definitions_3d()
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The create_kernel_definitions_2d() and create_kernel_definitions_3d() should usually be very similar, so
only one with unspecified dimension is described here.

The create_kernel_definitions() function has three main parts: setting template and function arguments,
generating strings containing the C++ code, and writing to files.

## Setting Template and Function Parameters
The template parameters and function parameters are passed as individual lists to the parsing function.
Each element of the list is of type SyntaxTree.Argument. The parameters used for the Rusanov kernels are

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
template_parameters = [DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("NumberOfVolumesPerAxisInPatch", DSL.SyntaxTree.TInteger()),
                         DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("HaloSize", DSL.SyntaxTree.TInteger()),
                         DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("NumberOfUnknowns", DSL.SyntaxTree.TInteger()),
                         DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("NumberOfAuxiliaryVariables", DSL.SyntaxTree.TInteger()),
                         DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("EvaluateFlux", DSL.SyntaxTree.TBoolean()),
                         DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("EvaluateNonconservativeProduct", DSL.SyntaxTree.TBoolean()),
                         DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("EvaluateSource", DSL.SyntaxTree.TBoolean()),
                         DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("EvaluateMaximumEigenvalueAfterTimeStep", DSL.SyntaxTree.TBoolean())]

  functor_arguments = [DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("flux", DSL.SyntaxTree.TCustom("const FluxFunctor&")),
                       DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("nonconservativeProduct", DSL.SyntaxTree.TCustom("const NonconservativeProductFunctor&")),
                       DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("sourceTerm", DSL.SyntaxTree.TCustom("const SourceFunctor&")),
                       DSL.SyntaxTree.Argument("maxEigenvalue", DSL.SyntaxTree.TCustom("const MaxEigenvalueFunctor&"))]
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

## Generating Function Strings
A Python string is generated using Parser().parse(timestepFunction, template_parameters, functor_arguments, namespaces, is_stateless, use_accelerator)
Its parameters are:
- timestepFunction: the function that defines the kernel
- template_parameters: as previously defined
- functor_arguments: as previously defined
- namespaces: a list of strings, each specifying a layer of a namespace. The generated function is defined to be 
within this namespace.
- is_stateless: a boolean which defaults to False. If the generated code is stateless, a template parameter is added 
at the start to specify a solver. Functor calls are removed from the function parameters, and calls to them are replaced
with calls to static alternatives.
- use_accelerator: a boolean which defaults to False. If True, an integer targetDevice parameter is added.

Calling this function returns the root node to an abstract syntax tree representing the kernel. The C++ code can then be
generated by calling its print methods. The options currently are print_cpp(), print_omp() and print_tree(), which does
not produce valid code and is for debugging only. The FunctionDefinition node, which is the root node, also has functions
for generating declarations for code organisation (print_declaration()). It also can generate functions with a timer for 
benchmarking (print_declaration_with_timer(), print_definition_with_timer()).

## Writing to Files
From here the process is standard Python. For consistency the generated files should be within a kernels folder. It is 
also recommended to use separate header files for declarations, and separate files for different dimensions. Certain
include files are also required as seen in the following example.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
if not os.path.exists("kernels"):
      os.makedirs("kernels")
  
  file = open("kernels/rusanov3d.h", "w")
  file.write(f"""#pragma once
#include "exahype2/CellData.h"
#include "exahype2/VolumeIndex.h"
#include "exahype2/fv/PatchUtils.h"
#include "exahype2/fv/rusanov/Functors.h"
#include "peano4/utils/Loop.h"
#include "tarch/timing/Measurement.h"
#include "tarch/timing/Watch.h"
             
{rusanov_kernel_declaration}
{rusanov_call_with_measurement_declaration}
{rusanov_stateless_kernel_declaration}
{rusanov_stateless_call_with_measurement_declaration}
{rusanov_accelerator_kernel_declaration}
{rusanov_accelerator_call_with_measurement_declaration}

#if defined(GPUOffloadingOMP)
{rusanov_omp_accelerator_kernel_declaration}
{rusanov_omp_accelerator_call_with_measurement_declaration}
#endif

#include "rusanov3d.cpph"
""")
  file.close()
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# Generating C++ Function Calls
The solver also needs to know how to call the new kernels, which should also be defined
in kernels.py. A function is defined which returns a Python string containing the C++
code used to call the timestep function. It checks which version of the function is
required, such as whether its stateless or uses an accelerator and returns the relevant
call.

If the code isn't stateless there are usually functor parameters which have to be passed in.
There are also template parameters which need to be determined which specify whether 
certain parts of the kernel are required. This is best seen by example in
create_compute_Riemann_kernel_for_Rusanov_dsl() in kernels.py.

*/